    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, it is absolutely possible to write a character who uses logic and counter-arguments, but it works effectively only if their reasoning is constructed from first principles rather than statistical trends. The key distinction is that their logic becomes valid because it rests on foundational human rights or historical inevitabilities, which sound pragmatic to their generation but feel abstract to the older politician. If the character also demonstrates this internal consistency, it avoids making them seem \"crazy\" while still highlighting the generational gap in data interpretation. The most compelling tension often arises when the young activist couples rigorous logic with an emotional appeal that the older generation dismisses as naive, creating a stronger dynamic than simple shouting matches.",
